qjs.wasm and qjswasm/ are unchanged from v0.0.6. The upstream *_test.go files
were not copied (they need testify); fork_test.go covers the changes below.

Changes made for Flats (search for "FORK (flats)"):

1. runtime.go: wazero >= 1.12 binds function type IDs to one runtime's store,
   so upstream (which compiled qjs.wasm once in a throwaway runtime and
   instantiated it in every new runtime) fails with "signature mismatch" on
   wazero v1.12.0. Every Runtime now compiles qjs.wasm inside its own wazero
   runtime, through a process-wide shared wazero.CompilationCache (or a
   per-directory file cache when Option.CacheDir is set), so only the first
   compile per configuration is slow.
2. runtime.go: the wazero RuntimeConfig is built per New. CloseOnContextDone
   is therefore a per-runtime setting; upstream cached the config of the first
   New in the process, so a first runtime without CloseOnContextDone silently
   disabled timeouts for every later one.
3. options.go/runtime.go: new Option.MemoryLimitPages caps the wasm linear
   memory (64 KiB pages; 0 means DefaultMemoryLimitPages = 1024 = 64 MiB).
   QuickJS's own MemoryLimit does not bound every allocation.
4. options.go/runtime.go: Option.CWD (default os.Getwd(), mounted read-write
   at "/") was replaced by Option.ReadOnlyDir: no file system at all by
   default, or one host directory mounted read-only at "/".
5. options.go: Stdout/Stderr default to io.Discard instead of the host
   process's os.Stdout/os.Stderr.
6. runtime.go: Close also closes the per-runtime wazero runtime.
7. stack.go (new) + runtime.go: qjs.wasm's 64 KiB C stack sits directly above
   its static data and QuickJS's stack check is compiled out under WASI, so
   ~250 levels of JS recursion silently corrupted the runtime. The module
   bytes are patched in memory to export the only global (__stack_pointer,
   verified to be the single mutable i32 global), and each Runtime moves its
   stack into a dedicated malloc'd block (Option.StackSize, default 1 MiB)
   whose bottom 64 KiB is a canary; Runtime.StackIntact reports whether a call
   reached it. qjs.wasm itself is not modified on disk.
8. runtime.go: guest sleeps (WASI poll_oneoff clock waits behind os.sleep,
   os.setTimeout and the std event loop) use a context-aware sleep that
   returns when Option.Context is done. Upstream used wazero's host sleep,
   which ignores the context, so os.sleep(3600000) blocked a call for an hour
   regardless of CloseOnContextDone.
9. runtime.go: WASI random_get reads crypto/rand instead of wazero's default
   fixed-seed source, which returns the same bytes in every runtime.

Usage note (unchanged upstream behaviour): every wasm call passes the
Runtime's *Context (which embeds a context.Context) to wazero, and with
CloseOnContextDone wazero reads ctx.Done() from a per-call goroutine that may
outlive the call. Assigning Runtime.Context().Context between calls is
therefore a data race; pass a stable Option.Context whose Done/Err delegate to
an atomically swapped per-request context instead (internal/runtime does).
